= Project Communication Management 
== Project Communication Management

Project Communication Management is a collection of processes that ensure the right information is sent, received, and understood by the right people. Without proper communication, ICT projects may face delays, errors, and even failure.

=== Importance of Effective Communication
+ *Clarity:* Everyone understands project goals, tasks, and responsibilities.
+ *Coordination:* Team members can work together properly without confusion.
+ *Problem-Solving:* Problems can be identified and solved quickly.
+ *Transparency:* Stakeholders are kept informed about project progress and changes.
+ *Success:* Good communication builds trust and increases the chance of project success.

=== Contents of a Communication Management Plan
+ *Purpose & Objectives:* Define why communication is needed.
+ *Stakeholder Information:* Identify who needs what type of information.
+ *Communication Methods:* Decide how information will be shared (meetings, emails, reports).
+ *Frequency & Timing:* Decide how often and when information will be shared.
+ *Responsibilities:* Define who will send and receive information.
+ *Format & Technology:* Decide the format, templates, software, and communication channels to use.
+ *Feedback & Escalation:* Define how feedback and problems will be handled.

=== Role of Communication in Successful Project Completion
- *Planning:* Shares project plans, timelines, and responsibilities.
- *Execution:* Keeps the team aligned through regular updates.
- *Monitoring:* Reports progress, risks, cost, and quality status.
- *Decision-Making:* Accurate information supports timely decisions.
- *Closure:* Documents lessons learned and prepares final reports.

== Information Distribution & Performance Reporting

=== Information Distribution
Information Distribution is the process of sharing project information with the right stakeholders at the right time.
*Tools and Techniques:*
+ *Meetings:* Face-to-face or online discussions.
+ *Reports:* Progress reports, status reports, and dashboards.
+ *Emails and Memos:* Used to quickly share updates.
+ *Project Management Software:* Tools such as MS Project, Jira, and Trello.
+ *Presentations:* Used to give visual information and summaries.

=== Tools and Techniques for Performance Reporting
+ *Status Reports:* Give updates about project progress, milestones, and problems.
+ *Progress Reports:* Show completed tasks, ongoing work, and future activities.
+ *Variance Analysis:* Compares planned performance with actual performance.
+ *Trend Analysis:* Studies past performance to predict future project trends.
+ *Dashboards and Visual Reports:* Use graphs, charts, and tables to show important information clearly.
+ *Meetings and Presentations:* Explain project performance directly to stakeholders.

=== Steps to Conduct an Effective Meeting
+ Check if the meeting is necessary.
+ Set clear purpose and goals.
+ Decide who should attend (invite only necessary people).
+ Share the agenda in advance.
+ Prepare materials (handouts, slides).
+ Start on time.
+ Run the meeting efficiently.
+ Build good relationships and encourage participation.

== Project Communication Management Processes 

=== Main Processes
+ *Communications Planning:* Decide what information is needed, who needs it, and how often.
+ *Information Distribution:* Share required information with the right people at the right time.
+ *Performance Reporting:* Collect and share information about project progress and problems.
+ *Administrative Closure:* Prepare and share final documents to formally close the project/phase.

*How to Write an Effective Email (3 Marks) *
\
+ Send to the right people only.
+ Write a clear subject line.
+ Keep it short and focused.
+ Use attachments carefully (keep them small).
+ Be safe (delete junk, avoid suspicious emails).
+ Protect with updated antivirus software.
+ Reply and organize quickly.
+ Use email features (CC, BCC, folders, filters) properly.


